# TypeScript/JavaScript (Node.js) testing

Test layout, runner and determinism conventions for Node.js server/library/
CLI code. Discover the project's actual runner before writing anything --
`node:test`, Vitest, Jest and `bun test` all exist in active use and are
not interchangeable in syntax or config.

## Layout and runner

- Discover the runner from `package.json`'s `scripts.test`, `devDependencies`,
  and any `vitest.config.*`/`jest.config.*` before writing a test --
  `describe`/`it`/`test` globals differ in import source (`node:test`,
  `vitest`, or Jest's ambient globals) even though the call shape looks
  similar.
- Match the project's existing test location convention: co-located
  `*.test.ts`/`*.spec.ts` next to the source file, or a parallel `test/`/
  `__tests__/` tree -- do not introduce the other layout alongside it.
- For `node:test`, run with `node --test` (or the project's npm script
  wrapping it); it needs no extra dependency and ships in Node itself.

## Fixtures and mocking

- Mock at the process boundary (network calls, the filesystem, a DB
  client, `Date.now`/timers) -- not an internal function one module over,
  which only checks that two mocks agree with each other.
- Use the runner's own timer mocking (`node:test`'s `mock.timers`,
  Vitest's `vi.useFakeTimers()`, Jest's `jest.useFakeTimers()`) for any
  test that depends on `setTimeout`/`setInterval`/`Date.now` -- a test
  that waits on a real timer is slow and, under load, flaky.
- Use `node:test`'s `mock.fn()`/`mock.method()` (or the runner's
  equivalent) instead of hand-rolled spy objects when the runner ships
  one already.

## Determinism

- Async tests must `await` every assertion-relevant promise; a test
  function that returns before its assertions run reports green
  regardless of what the assertions found.
- Pass the test's own `AbortSignal` (`t.signal` in `node:test`, or the
  runner's equivalent) into any `fetch`/cancelable call a test makes, so a
  test that times out does not leave the request running past it.
- Never depend on test execution order or on state a previous test left
  behind (a shared in-memory array, a module-level counter) -- each test
  sets up and tears down its own state.
- A flaky test (timing-, network-, or order-dependent) gets fixed at the
  dependency it is racing, not wrapped in a retry or a longer timeout.

## Coverage expectations

- Every new/changed exported function, class method, and CLI command gets
  at least: one happy-path case, one edge case (empty/`null`/`undefined`/
  boundary value), and one error case that asserts the thrown error type
  or message, not just that something threw.
- A bug fix ships with a regression test that fails on the pre-fix code
  and passes after -- without one, the fix has no evidence it addressed
  the reported behavior.
